    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The core of the Mau Mau strategy was often **guerrilla warfare,** favoring targeted, often highly ideological, attacks against loyalist and colonial strongholds in the central and western parts of the country. For the fighters, these direct actions were a radical assertion of **political sovereignty and a defense of their traditional land and community rights** against the perceived complete dispossession by the colonial power.\n\nThe British, in response, overwhelmingly favored **brutal and comprehensive counter-insurgency tactics,** which involved a widespread, divisive \"pipeline of loyal support\" and the devastating use of **'savage right' (political and often forced \"villagization\") and systemic force against the perceived political \"unruly\" population more broadly,** not just the armed combatants.\n\nThis dynamic is critical: the **insurgents fought for political and land justice,** while the **regime used extreme force to enforce absolute political and social control,** often devastating the very civilian support base the resistance needed to survive.",
